WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:08.309
Pretty sure you know at this point I'm a small
government guy.

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:13.690
What small government means to me economically,
is you should keep most of what you earn,

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:18.510
and you yourself are the best arbiter to decide
where you money goes.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:22.939
Whether the money goes to fixing your house
or buying a new car or to funding Planned

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:27.599
Parenthood or supporting Lincoln Center, it's
up to you.

00:00:27.599 --> 00:00:32.119
You've worked hard for your money and I believe
you are a better judge of how to spend it

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:36.740
rather than giving it to a wasteful government,
who will often spend it on programs you don't

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:38.240
even care about.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:43.310
At the same time, being for small government
isn't only about taxes and economics.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:48.830
At its root, being for small governement is
also being for the individual.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:54.580
I believe your faculty to understand how you
want to live, is far better than anyone else

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:57.450
telling you how to live your life.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:02.530
At the same time, I understand the need for
the government is different if you live in

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:05.890
a rural area or in an urban center.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:10.910
In a rural area with less people, there is
most likely a lesser need for regulation than

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:12.190
there is in an urban area.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:15.160
Take something as simple as noise ordinances.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:21.070
If you live in a remote rural area, there
is most likely no need for a law to stop people

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:24.950
from doing construction at night or blasting
music too loudly.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:29.720
But if you live in an urban center, say New
York City, where people live in small apartments

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:33.630
on top of one another, a noise ordinance might
be important.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.730
You can't have construction people knocking
down walls at all hours of the night, and

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:40.630
you might not want your neighbor blasting
music at 3 am.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:44.590
The next piece of the limited government puzzle
puzzle is does the government have to be involved

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:47.110
so many facets of our life at all?

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:50.909
Even in the case of New York City with it's
cramped conditions and tiny living spaces,

00:01:50.909 --> 00:01:56.590
is it the government who has to tell companies
not to do construction or tenants not to blast

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:58.990
music?

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:01.380
This is where an interesting discussion can
happen.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:08.409
Is it the job of the government, as laid out
by the constitution to regulate these interpersonal

00:02:08.409 --> 00:02:09.409
issues?

00:02:09.409 --> 00:02:13.690
Or is it the job of individuals to deal with
these issues themselves?

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:18.409
Should a building in NYC have a policy about
noise, instead of leaving it to the government?

00:02:18.409 --> 00:02:22.620
Should it be incumbent on you to talk to your
neighbor about the volume of their music,

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.639
rather than leaving it to the government?

00:02:24.639 --> 00:02:29.159
Can issues we think of traditionally being
dealt with by the government actually be dealt

00:02:29.159 --> 00:02:31.629
with in the private sector?

00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:36.260
Is the government already to deep into controlling
us that we could never scale it back without

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:37.969
burning down the system?

00:02:37.969 --> 00:02:43.389
While noise ordinances aren't the most pressing
issue out there, I use it as a simple example

00:02:43.389 --> 00:02:48.139
of the battle between the private and the
public and how sometimes we assign power to

00:02:48.139 --> 00:02:52.940
the government when perhaps there could be
a better option based in our own capacity

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.379
to reason for ourselves.

00:02:55.379 --> 00:02:58.819
With all this in mind, we're continuing our
partnership with Learn Liberty this week and

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:04.280
joining me is best selling author and economist
at George Mason University, Bryan Caplan.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:09.670
Bryan is an anarcho-capitalist, which is in
simple terms is an extreme libertarian who

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:11.680
wants government pretty much out of everything.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.659
We'll talk about taxes, healthcare, rent control
and much more and see if there are places

00:03:16.659 --> 00:03:22.569
that private can do a better job than what
the public has been able to do.

